--TEST--
Every `Core\Task::all` field answers what its own callable declares, so an anonymous function, a method reference, a shape held in a variable and a bare `callable` all compile and only the last one widens — `rule:concurrency/all-answers-a-typed-shape`, `rule:types/callable-signature`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `Core\Task::all`'s result is a shape carrying each field's own return type,
// and `rule:types/callable-signature` is what puts that type within a *type's*
// reach: the field is read off the argument's type rather than off the
// expression written at the call, so where the callable was written stopped
// mattering.
//
// This is the agreement shape (`docs/agent/conventions.md`): four different
// ways of writing a callable, asked one question each, so a checker that grew
// a special case for one of them fails here while still looking right on the
// others.
class Held {
    public static function job(): int {
        return 7;
    }
}

// An anonymous function and a method reference both carry a signature, so both
// fields are typed by what that signature returns.
var $page = Core\Task::all({
    literal:    fn(): int => 1,
    firstClass: Held::job(...),
});
int $one = $page->literal;
int $seven = $page->firstClass;
echo $one + $seven, "\n";

// A variable holding the whole shape is as good as one written at the call.
var $set = {rows: fn(): array<int> => [1, 2, 3]};
var $held = Core\Task::all($set);
array<int> $rows = $held->rows;
echo Core\Arr::count($rows), "\n";

// Bare `callable` is the top of the lattice and declares no result, so this
// field — and this field alone — answers `mixed`, which is what every other
// position pays for the same omission.
callable $loader = fn(): int => 4;
var $loose = Core\Task::all({unsigned: $loader, signed: fn(): string => "ok"});
string $signed = $loose->signed;
echo $signed, "\n";
echo $loose->unsigned as int, "\n";
--EXPECT--
8
3
ok
4
